hi there today i am going to talk about  what is the easiest language to learn  because it's a question that comes up  all the time  and there i don't think there is a  simple answer because it depends on a  number of factors but i'm going to go  over what i consider to be  the factors that influence what makes  languages easy or difficult to learn  the first thing is to me  how many words in the new language are  similar to words that you already know  from your own language or from another  language all right  there's a thing called lexical distance  and you can google and you'll find a  number of sites that provide information  what this means is how close is the  vocabulary in language a to language b  all right so if you're an english  speaker  i use one of these websites to check  the closest language in terms of  vocabulary is friesian which is some  form of dutch but generally speaking  dutch and swedish are the closest to  english in vocabulary then comes german  then comes french and other  romance languages now i haven't looked  into all of this you know sufficiently  well but i would imagine that this  lexical difference  that people normally talk about refers  to the most commonly used words  because in english the most commonly  used words are going to be  more  the higher percentage of sort of  germanic type words go  come eat speak and so forth  uh when you get into more difficult  content you're reading on politics or  history or science then you get a higher  percentage of latin or in the case of  english french origin words so in  english for example 60 or so of the  vocabulary is actually a french origin  so  so again if we're talking lexical  distance here  theoretically swedish german is easier  uh closer but i i think if you are at a  higher level of your reading i suspect  that english is closer to french because  of the number of french import words  that come from the norman conquest  so that's one thing is the the sort of  lexical proximity if you're a spanish  speaker and you go to learn portuguese  basically you have 95 of the words uh  the same is true for  uh you know a swedish person learning  danish or a mandarin speaker learning  cantonese  uh once we move to the slavic languages  they're not quite so close in terms of  lexical proximity in other words  similarity of vocabulary  uh  you know russian is sort of the outlier  and languages like ukrainian polish  czech slovak  are closer  i don't know  i don't have information on the other  slavic languages this column the  southern slavic languages  so obviously that's one consideration is  how close the vocabulary is  uh the second consideration is  uh  structure so german may have a lot of  common vocabulary with english  but the word order is quite different so  swedish is actually a lot easier  to me i think than german  because it takes us a while even though  we understand theoretically that okay  the verb comes at the end but actually  to get the brain used to this uh  different structure i think is a bit of  an obstacle so similarity and structure  can be a help for example  as a person like me who speaks chinese  and japanese i have an advantage in  learning korean because 50 of the  vocabulary in korean is of chinese  origin and the structure at least the  word order is very similar to  japanese so these are all things that  influence what makes a language easy  but there are other factors for example  how much you know do you like the  language or even before that  what sort of  a learning content is available  uh because  it's important to have material that is  of interest to you and just a little bit  difficult to help you along until you  can actually start using authentic  material so very often it's the  availability of that sort of  intermediate range of learning content  having uh material where you have both  the audio and the text  so for example  uh i am learning greek now i would say  quicker than any language that i've  attempted to learn here i am i've  basically put five months into it and i  had a conversation the other day we're  talking about politics i can't say that  i'm fluent not by a long stretch but i'm  expressing myself much better than i was  able to do in korean after much more  effort or in check after much more  effort  uh and that is in the case of czech  after i had learned russian  and was speaking russian you know fairly  well and yet with with check i had all  kinds of trouble even though check is  written in the latin alphabet and that's  another consideration if you have to  confront a different writing system that  makes it more difficult it is always  easier for me to read in the alpha in  the latin alphabet than in the cyrillic  alphabet although i have read millions  of words in the cyrillic  alphabet  uh so but with check we didn't have a  lot of good content at link  and so it was always too much of a jump  almost to get me up to where i could  really take advantage of some of the  great material that the czech radio or  chesky roslas offered  the same with korean i didn't have  enough of that intermediate stuff and i  think that's where  the mini stories that i'm using for  greek have been so tremendous because in  fact greek should be more difficult for  me it has  it is much more remote in terms of  lexical proximity proximity vocabulary  to any of the languages that i have  learned it's written in a different  alphabet  it has a few  structural  problem column issues that i have to  learn but i'm learning it very quickly i  feel wow and i think it's the effect of  these many stories 60 of them which i  listened to repeatedly and that i was  able to get into and these many stories  use a lot of the day-to-day vocabulary  especially verbs which i think is very  important so to summarize here it's the  lexical proximity in other words how  similar is the vocabulary  uh and then to some extent the structure  uh  second of all it's the availability of a  comprehensible input in other words uh  meaningful things that you want to  listen to and read  in the language that's that is not too  difficult  for you uh because i think at the at the  upper end the truly difficult material  it's usually possible to find stuff  audio books newspapers newspapers are  easier actually but audiobooks with  ebooks and stuff like that these things  can often be found  but it's getting you up to that level  that's going to influence how easy or  difficult that language is to learn and  finally another important point in what  makes a language difficult or easy and  and and this relates also to a question  that i often get which is what is your  favorite language  the issue here of course is how much do  you like the language if you like the  language if you are motivated to learn  the language for whatever reason if you  don't resist the language you don't find  the language stupid because they say  things differently  uh you don't get frustrated because it  sounds like it's just one big mumble if  you have none of these negative feelings  about the language and you are very  positive and and motivated and you love  the language that's going to make it  easier  and i i was sort of reminded of this as  i was reading my  uh  greek podcast which i listened to and  then i read about greek history ancient  greek history and it talks about the  parthenon and it talks about athens and  it talked about the citizens in athens  and then the  metis meticos  who were basically foreigners who lived  in athens but didn't have the same  rights as citizens and then i said where  have i heard that before it seems to me  there was a song  called le metec by mustaqi  and so then i went to youtube  and i googled  le maytec by mustaqi i'm not going to  sing it because i'm not a very good  singer and i listened to it and i said  to myself you know i really love french  french was the first sort of new second  language that i really went after and  and became fluent in  and i think if i'm asked which language  do i prefer i would have to say  i love them all when i'm learning them i  love my greek i love my ukrainian  russian chinese korean i love them all  but my first love and probably still my  favorite language is french and i'm  gonna leave a link here  to uh  lumaytec by george mustacky and i  encourage you to listen to it and if you  fall in love with french then you'll  have an easier time  learning it thank you for listening bye  for now  [Music]  you
 

